    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. i'm a bit worried about the blood meal though, since it's such a heavy hitter. how much of a risk is there for nitrogen leaching if we get a bunch of heavy rain like we usually do this time of year? also, is there any way the soil microbes might react poorly to that sudden influx of organic matter?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "While blood meal is potent, proper soil incorporation will lessen the risk of rapid leaching during heavy rain. To mitigate this, consider incorporating the blood meal deeply into the top few inches of soil rather than just spreading it on top. The soil microbes generally thrive on nitrogen, so a sudden influx can actually stimulate microbial activity, though managing *too* sudden a spike is the concern. Incorporating it helps provide a more stable supply for the microbes to utilize gradually.",
      "source": "target",
